Dark Souls 3 is a game of valleys and  Peaks down through dungeons and up over  castle walls  it's a plummet into places we shouldn't  be and escape from places we don't  belong but of course we fight our way  through and find our way out there are a  few stumbles during the ascent but in  the end Dark Souls 3 is well worth the  riveting climb  this is the third in a series of dark  fence and role-playing games known for  their brutal difficulty in unforgiving  nature it grants minimal direction and  little room for error as with its  predecessors playing Dark Souls 3 means  accepting to extremes  recurring defeat and the reward of  breaking through it this is a game that  recognizes the value of perseverance  tearing you down before it pulls you  back up reinforcing the spots where it  broke you preparing you for the next  Valley down the road the world itself is  a disconnected series of detailed areas  some sprawl outward while others stack  on top of themselves folding back and  looping around in intricate webs it's a  testament to the impeccable level design  that discovering a bonfire checkpoint is  as important as leveling your character  or defeating a challenging boss in this  dangerous world of swamps prisons and  undead villages every milestone is a  victory it's also impressive how Dark  Souls 3 strikes a balance between  exploration and guidance there's usually  more than one path you can take through  the world at any time to new bosses  secret dungeons or new areas entirely  but never too many that it feels  overwhelming  the level design encourages wandering  without losing focus  obstacles come in a variety of grotesque  forms along the way hulking Giants and  feral dogs ancient Knights and suicidal  monks each enemy is a unique threat and  in groups they grow more dynamic and  more punishing demanding a flexible  approach to combat Dark Souls 3 is also  clever in the way it plays on your false  sense of comfort many deaths come at you  when you underestimate an enemy Hill  dozens of times before the danger is  always lurking it lends an added tension  to the world around you in the long run  survival requires patience  this is true throughout there's a  certain cadence to the combat a certain  pattern to each enemy that's only  discernible when you take a time to  observe it some enemies are weak near  their sword on others are vulnerable  from behind instinct may tell you to  dodge every time a knight retract its  spear but wasting stamina could lead to  a quick death  forcing a restart at the most recent  bonfire Dark Souls 3 doesn't just teach  you new skills it forces you to forget  ones you've already learned the combat  fluctuates between measured duels and  frantic fights but it almost always  manages to keep things fair you may be  outnumbered and underpowered but defeat  is usually your fault  sometimes however Dark Souls three  breaks that rule the camera often  struggles to adjust in tight spaces and  the lock-on mechanic can be capricious  especially against Dark Souls threes  more mobile aggressive enemies in boss  fights that require precision and  emphasize camera becomes all the more of  a hindrance but what impressive  monstrosity these bosses can be in fact  several display more creativity than any  others in developer from software's RPG  lineup these creatures play on your  expectations and force you to adapt one  boss fight pits you against a crowd of  pyromancers that inches toward you  hinting at its weakness with subtle  visual cues intuition tells you to keep  your distance but it soon becomes clear  you'll need to enter the fray it rips  you out of your comfort zone at a  harrowing pace despite the nuance and  novelty of most bosses however some  stick to familiar ground deja boo' kicks  in during several boss fights when the  monsters display movesets similar to  those that came before them diminishing  the creativity displayed elsewhere these  enemies feel recycled it feel repetitive  the skin may be different but the Beast  remains the same  Dark Souls 3 is at its best when it  rewards your progress and test your  characters hard-earned growth these  redundant bosses fly in the face of the  skills you've learned they repeat  patterns you've already mastered they  remind you of a point earlier in your  journey when you weren't nearly as good  as you are now but by and large your  growth is very much respected  it's that thread that near-constant  sense of progress that leads to Dark  Souls three's greatest moments we create  our travelers we make them stronger  faster more resilient turning them into  fighters as we to learn the intricacies  of this foreboding world we can't slay  the final boss until we conquer every  enemy before it so by the end of Dark  Souls 3 we've truly mastered something  that's a special feeling the story here  is more concerned with tone than  exposition but what plot there is tell  the tale of pawns rising from obscurity  the old Lords have abandoned their posts  and in the hunt to usurp them we descend  into those dark valleys and climb those  imposing peaks this is the essence of  Dark Souls 3 periods of doubt followed  by great reward the journey may be rocky  but there's a throne waiting at the end  nameless accursed undead unfit  even to be cinder  and so it is  the - seeketh embers  you  you
 

